{"authored" [{"question" "q-75829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{'}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{'}(3).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" "category" "local_reconstruction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/questions.json" "file_sha256" "50327f088e1802526fef61aecff55d85426669a0cf8ea00def90f5806b3d5934" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/database-import/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "735387c3560cc206e9e6dc540884b5be0d105253f5d3c2618709fce223388e6b" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/database-import/verification.json" "basis" 552} {"question" "q-75829" "field" "answer" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "local_reconstruction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/repairs.json" "file_sha256" "0328005daa91919dd8bb81fd729228440954ec812e808e91c99da77efc57aee9" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "571ee7e4f8997fdfae74f800edd84d0efce760607b5043aa85a8d9d81962095f" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/verification.json" "basis" 653} {"question" "q-75829" "field" "answer" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "local_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/repairs.json" "file_sha256" "0328005daa91919dd8bb81fd729228440954ec812e808e91c99da77efc57aee9" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "571ee7e4f8997fdfae74f800edd84d0efce760607b5043aa85a8d9d81962095f" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/verification.json" "basis" 653} {"question" "q-75829" "field" "answer" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "local_authored" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/repairs.json" "file_sha256" "0328005daa91919dd8bb81fd729228440954ec812e808e91c99da77efc57aee9" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "571ee7e4f8997fdfae74f800edd84d0efce760607b5043aa85a8d9d81962095f" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/verification.json" "basis" 653} {"question" "q-75829" "field" "answer" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "− (1)/(5)"] "category" "local_authored" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/repairs.json" "file_sha256" "0328005daa91919dd8bb81fd729228440954ec812e808e91c99da77efc57aee9" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "571ee7e4f8997fdfae74f800edd84d0efce760607b5043aa85a8d9d81962095f" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/verification.json" "basis" 653} {"question" "q-75829" "field" "answer" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1)/(3)"] "category" "local_authored" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/repairs.json" "file_sha256" "0328005daa91919dd8bb81fd729228440954ec812e808e91c99da77efc57aee9" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "571ee7e4f8997fdfae74f800edd84d0efce760607b5043aa85a8d9d81962095f" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/verification.json" "basis" 653} {"question" "q-75829" "field" "answer" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "− 3"] "category" "local_authored" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/repairs.json" "file_sha256" "0328005daa91919dd8bb81fd729228440954ec812e808e91c99da77efc57aee9" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "571ee7e4f8997fdfae74f800edd84d0efce760607b5043aa85a8d9d81962095f" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/verification.json" "basis" 653} {"question" "q-342579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and the point $P(1,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc2c2911a5a6dd4b35e3b00a44af44748a7cbaee5a875f5c4b2bc96dc1bc02f9" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot {({x}^{2}+1)}^{′}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}+2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1+2x-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1+2(1)-{1}^{2}}{{({1}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc2c2911a5a6dd4b35e3b00a44af44748a7cbaee5a875f5c4b2bc96dc1bc02f9" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc2c2911a5a6dd4b35e3b00a44af44748a7cbaee5a875f5c4b2bc96dc1bc02f9" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc2c2911a5a6dd4b35e3b00a44af44748a7cbaee5a875f5c4b2bc96dc1bc02f9" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc2c2911a5a6dd4b35e3b00a44af44748a7cbaee5a875f5c4b2bc96dc1bc02f9" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc644e02aa9c85304749abccec144ff0b1ac05836a7caf34f8e8f3110207947d" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/state.json" "file_sha256" "bc2c2911a5a6dd4b35e3b00a44af44748a7cbaee5a875f5c4b2bc96dc1bc02f9" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "ffc1cea693eadebca1d0722193a9d3e73952f795fc203f350270288f864689b7" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "ffc1cea693eadebca1d0722193a9d3e73952f795fc203f350270288f864689b7" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "0cdc319106ac8db98fe7eea74bc885352729f401077493776898eaf46a879804" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "0cdc319106ac8db98fe7eea74bc885352729f401077493776898eaf46a879804" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "7498192180fc73b1bdd062e14c0278baa3f43e019b4ac0b34c49325d0038a118" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "7498192180fc73b1bdd062e14c0278baa3f43e019b4ac0b34c49325d0038a118" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "ed2f86fa3614aef04eaeed13dfadf4132c5147827078633f40125a7cb6af2d3e" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "ed2f86fa3614aef04eaeed13dfadf4132c5147827078633f40125a7cb6af2d3e" "basis" 849 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=9-\\frac{2}{3}x$ and the point $P(3,7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =9-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(3,7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(7,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a648ab828e2f48069d87e8219def2436ba09debad65a62452aba8b1413b68ff0" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=-2$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a648ab828e2f48069d87e8219def2436ba09debad65a62452aba8b1413b68ff0" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "c80e0edeb5d624c34619ec70ca5f0448c6d09c5fb05a71d281a2f4ce62b3083a" "basis" 933 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-342562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A\\,(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342562" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-18601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/7e881b5cb3613ff72264739b1cbe1214c1041a78d201cfc323476910e7ce8e3c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(4,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-18601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $(2,4)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $1$, hence, ${f}^{′}(4)=1.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-18601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-18601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-18601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-18601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/feb02396a635c3f416b07383b03269e12fde738da3345aa6d6d9fe5350745746.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0.5)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0.5,1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4},$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B\\,(0.5,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0.5)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0.5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-342570" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342570" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ln(4)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312578" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-15709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/0d7c1b55cbc73a769f7ab0c0c8e634d54356eb36e77dc7ca0d06ff77d9367162.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(2,-1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-15709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B\\,(2,-1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-15709" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-15709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-15709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-15709" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+{2}^{x}$ and the point $P(0,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,0)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+{2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x+{2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-312580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312580" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{\\ln(2)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and the point $P(1,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(x-1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot {({x}^{2}+1)}^{′}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-(x-1)\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}+2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1+2x-{x}^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1+2(1)-{1}^{2}}{{({1}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342579" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=9-\\frac{2}{3}x$ and the point $P(3,7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =9-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(3,7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(7,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and the point $P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342582" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-342582" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342582" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-75831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $-6$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ | $5$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-75831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=1$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=-6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-6)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-75831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-75831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-75831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-75831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=3$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=-2$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-75829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-75829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=5$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-75829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-75829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-75829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{9}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-75829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342596" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2x+1 & =1 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+2x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-76114" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-110338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+10}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-110338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ x+10 & =4x+4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=2.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+10}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{9}{{(2+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{9} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-110338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-110338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-110338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-110338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(8)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(8).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(8)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(8) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =8 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4)({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(8)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-4x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4({2}^{3})-4(2) \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-312585" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-312585" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{24}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-x.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\le -1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\le -1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}-x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-342599" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-342599" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=1+2{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ 1+2{e}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(3)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =1+2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "de3935749b25242fed4a1988ac2589e3ca0585c675b8d81dfcfa47afe83fe8e4"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{e}^{-4}}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2{e}^{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "q-76128" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/state.json" "file_sha256" "2a45135769e9e6b2a2ae9e14439682f6ef0f9ab51ecd85f119a8f7079a4e1990"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "6927b7e2adc2a3da44fc320642804f51a712731526b6e6c6dbf52710b2f43ed0"} {"question" "e-14720" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14720.json" "file_sha256" "6927b7e2adc2a3da44fc320642804f51a712731526b6e6c6dbf52710b2f43ed0"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "0cdc319106ac8db98fe7eea74bc885352729f401077493776898eaf46a879804"} {"question" "e-14719" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14719.json" "file_sha256" "0cdc319106ac8db98fe7eea74bc885352729f401077493776898eaf46a879804"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "7498192180fc73b1bdd062e14c0278baa3f43e019b4ac0b34c49325d0038a118"} {"question" "e-14721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14721.json" "file_sha256" "7498192180fc73b1bdd062e14c0278baa3f43e019b4ac0b34c49325d0038a118"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "ed2f86fa3614aef04eaeed13dfadf4132c5147827078633f40125a7cb6af2d3e"} {"question" "e-14722" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/example-14722.json" "file_sha256" "ed2f86fa3614aef04eaeed13dfadf4132c5147827078633f40125a7cb6af2d3e"}] "usage" {"q-342596" [] "e-14721" [] "q-76114" [] "q-75829" [] "e-14719" [] "e-14722" [] "q-15641" [] "e-14720" [] "q-342589" [] "q-342579" []} "basis" 1253 "retractions" [[17592186261590 :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{'}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{'}(3).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |"] [17592186261590 :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=5$ and ${f}^{'}(5)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186261590 :question/answer-fields 17592186274991] [17592186064865 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{3} + 2x + 1$. Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})^{′}(1)$."] [17592186063913 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186063700 17592186063701 17592186063702 17592186063703 17592186063704 17592186273536 17592186273537 17592186273538 17592186273539 17592186273540 17592186279596 17592186279597 17592186279606 17592186279607 17592186283000 17592186283001 17592186283002 17592186283003 17592186283004 17592186283014] "immutable_fields" [[17592186063699 :answer-field/correct] [17592186063699 :answer-field/id] [17592186063699 :answer-field/key] [17592186063699 :answer-field/type] [17592186274991 :answer-field/correct] [17592186274991 :answer-field/id] [17592186274991 :answer-field/key] [17592186274991 :answer-field/type] [17592186279595 :answer-field/correct] [17592186279595 :answer-field/id] [17592186279595 :answer-field/key] [17592186279595 :answer-field/type] [17592186279605 :answer-field/correct] [17592186279605 :answer-field/id] [17592186279605 :answer-field/key] [17592186279605 :answer-field/type] [17592186282999 :answer-field/correct] [17592186282999 :answer-field/id] [17592186282999 :answer-field/key] [17592186282999 :answer-field/type] [17592186283013 :answer-field/correct] [17592186283013 :answer-field/id] [17592186283013 :answer-field/key] [17592186283013 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
